> 
> I have two matrices:
> 
> P[i,k] and X[i,s]

Umm...that would be two elements, not matrices?

> Define P[i,] and row i of P written as a column, and
> X[i,] as the i-th row of X written as a column.

I think it would be better to write actual S syntax rather than
redefining the existing one.  cbind(P[i,]) is a column.

> I would like to:
> 
> Sum over i of the kronecker product of P[i,] and X[i,]

I don't have S on this computer, so this is R code.  Hence, minor
changes might be required.

   foo <- function (P, X) {
       dimP <- dim(P)
       dimX <- dim(X)
       if (dimP[1] != dimX[1]) stop("P and X must have same number of rows")
       out <- matrix(0, dimP[2]*dimX[2])
       for (i in 1:dimP[i]) out <- out + cbind(P[i,]) %x% cbind(X[i,])
   }

> Sum over i of the kronecker product of P[i,]%*%P[i,] and X[i,]

You defined P[i,] to be a column.  But you can't take the matrix
product of an arbitrary column with itself.  Specify more clearly
what you want to achieve.

