A summary of the replies I received to the question "How does Stata compare 
with S-Plus?" is as follows:

"It's like the difference between a freezer well-stocked with tv dinners, 
and a pantry well stocked with raw ingredients...." [A. Dorfman]

"Stata is an excellent statistical package, and on the whole far easier to 
use than S-plus [...] The one gripe I have with Stata is the way it treats 
categorical covariates, which could be a little more straightforward" 
[Peter England]

"Stata [is] easier to use, but Splus has superior graphics and a number of 
statistical methodologies not implemented in Stata" [Murray Finkelstein]

" [...] the programming language for Stata does not look much like what you 
do when you run commands, and there is not the set of self-contained 
flexible data structures to support programming that you have in S-Plus. 
Basically you are working with columns in a worksheet. Also, the graphics 
are much more limited than in S " [Alan Zaslavsky]

"Stata is quite nice for non-visual data analysis. S-PLUS is nice for 
producing pretty pictures and programming (Stata has a macro language, but 
it's not really the same as a real programming language, which is what 
S-PLUS is)" [Tony Rossini]

