From dsmith at statsci.com  Mon Dec  4 16:36:12 2000
From: dsmith at statsci.com (David M Smith)
Date: Mon, 04 Dec 2000 08:36:12 -0800
Subject: RE: NAs and subsetting data frames
In-Reply-To: <p05001900b65166ce2c2e@[128.115.153.6]>
References: <p05001900b65166ce2c2e@[128.115.153.6]>
Message-ID: <LGEFIDJJDBKMHCAKBCOIAEMLCFAA.dsmith@splus.mathsoft.com>

This has been fixed in S-PLUS 6.  This is the new behaviour on both Windows
and Unix:

> tst <- data.frame(x = c(1:3, rep(NA, 3)), a = letters[1:6], y = 1:6)
> tst[tst$x == 1,  ]
     x  a  y
 X1  1  a  1
 NA NA NA NA
NA1 NA NA NA
NA2 NA NA NA

The all-NA rows are superfluous, but I believe this is the intended behavior
by way of comparison with vector selection, following the rule that the
result is always as long as the index vector[*].  For example:

> (1:3)[c(1, NA, 4, 3)]
[1]  1 NA NA  3

The result is a vector of length 4, even though it could be argued that S
could drop 1 or 2 of the elements (or return an error).

# David

[*] There is one exception to this rule, which I'll leave as an exercise for
the readers :)

