In actual fact the example given below is not for a double integral so much
as for a repeated integral in the old sense.  The same idea could be pushed
further to more levels of repeated integral, provided you could express your
region of integration by, for example, giving the lower and upper x-limits
as functions of (y, z, w), the lower and upper y-limits as functions of (z,
w), &c.  It would be tedious to do so and very, very slow to work so my
strong recommendation is that you abandon this idea and try some other.  

I seem to recall there is an R multiple integral library but I don't know
what is involved in porting it to S-PLUS (probably more than setting up R on
your system...).

